answer:
The 'switch' statement is a multi-way branch selection control statement that tests the value of an integer or character expression against a list of constant case labels.

Rules:
1. Expression must evaluate to integral (int, char, enum) type.
2. Case values must be constants or literal values; duplicate values are not allowed.
3. 'break' statement terminates execution of switch body and prevents fall-through.
4. 'default' executes if no case matches.

Example Program:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    char op;
    float a, b;
    cout << "Enter operator (+, -, *, /): ";
    cin >> op;
    cout << "Enter two numbers: ";
    cin >> a >> b;
    switch(op) {
        case '+': cout << a + b; break;
        case '-': cout << a - b; break;
        case '*': cout << a * b; break;
        case '/': cout << (b != 0 ? a/b : 0); break;
        default: cout << "Invalid operator";
    }
    return 0;
}

1. while Loop: Entry-controlled loop.
Syntax: while (condition) { statements; }
2. for Loop: Entry-controlled compact loop having initialization, condition, and update in one line.
Syntax: for (init; condition; update) { statements; }
3. do-while Loop: Exit-controlled loop executing at least once.
Syntax: do { statements; } while (condition);

Example Program:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    cout << "While loop: ";
    int i = 1; while (i <= 5) { cout << i << " "; i++; }
    cout << "\nFor loop: ";
    for (int j = 1; j <= 5; j++) { cout << j << " "; }
    cout << "\nDo-While loop: ";
    int k = 1; do { cout << k << " "; k++; } while (k <= 5);
    return 0;
}

